  "generated_description": "<div class=\"mountain-description prose prose-stone max-w-none\">\n  <p><strong>Jack Mountain</strong> is a 9,065-foot summit in Washington. With roughly 4,213 feet of topographic prominence, it stands well above the surrounding terrain.</p>\n  <h3>When to Go</h3><p>Late May through October is the main season. Snow and ice linger into spring and return early in fall at this latitude and elevation; in winter this becomes a different, more serious undertaking requiring traction devices and warm layers.</p>\n  <h3>Know Before You Go</h3><p>Download the GPX track from this page before you go \u2014 cell coverage is unreliable in the mountains and a saved route works offline in any GPS app. Conditions change: verify current access, closures and weather with the official land manager before you go. Trail data is sourced from OpenStreetMap contributors (ODbL).</p>\n  <p>Looking to link objectives or plan a weekend? Nearby summits include Mount Prophet, Mount Spickard and Buckner Mountain \u2014 each has its own guide on this site.</p>\n</div>",
